1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:04,935
As many of you here today have worked in the hotel industry for some

2
00:00:04,935 --> 00:00:13,100
time, I’m sure you have experienced the problem of high staff turnover in your

3
00:00:13,100 --> 00:00:13,150
hotels.

4
00:00:13,150 --> 00:00:19,962
Every hotel relies on having loyal and experienced members of staff who make sure

5
00:00:19,962 --> 00:00:22,390
that everything runs smoothly.

6
00:00:22,390 --> 00:00:28,340
If staff are constantly changing, it can make life difficult for everyone.

7
00:00:28,340 --> 00:00:34,020
But why do staff leave frequently in many hotels?

8
00:00:34,020 --> 00:00:41,380
Of course, many hotel jobs, such as cleaning, are low-skilled and are not well-paid.

9
00:00:41,380 --> 00:00:46,633
A lot of managers think it’s this and the long hours that are the

10
00:00:46,633 --> 00:00:52,447
main causes of high staff turnover – but what they don’t realise is that

11
00:00:52,447 --> 00:00:57,310
it’s the lack of training in many hotel jobs which is a huge

12
00:00:57,310 --> 00:00:57,680
factor.

13
00:00:57,680 --> 00:01:03,450
So, what kind of problems does a high turnover of staff cause?

14
00:01:03,450 --> 00:01:09,845
Well, having to recruit new staff all the time can be very time-consuming,

15
00:01:09,845 --> 00:01:15,157
and managers may have to cover some duties while waiting for new staff to

16
00:01:15,157 --> 00:01:16,240
arrive.

17
00:01:16,240 --> 00:01:22,051
This means they don’t have time to think about less immediate problems such as

18
00:01:22,051 --> 00:01:23,720
how to improve their service.

19
00:01:23,720 --> 00:01:30,490
When staff leave, it can also severely affect the colleagues they leave behind.

20
00:01:30,490 --> 00:01:33,603
It has a negative effect on remaining staff,

21
00:01:33,603 --> 00:01:37,900
who may start to feel that they too should be thinking about leaving.

22
00:01:37,900 --> 00:01:41,750
So, what can be done to change this situation?

23
00:01:41,750 --> 00:01:49,526
Firstly, managers should stop making basic errors which leave their staff feeling upset

24
00:01:49,526 --> 00:01:50,410
and resentful.

25
00:01:50,410 --> 00:01:58,090
When organising shifts, for example, make sure you never give certain staff preferential treatment.

26
00:01:58,090 --> 00:02:02,750
All staff should be given some choice about when they work,

27
00:02:02,750 --> 00:02:07,410
and everyone should have to work some evening and weekend shifts.

28
00:02:07,410 --> 00:02:12,775
If you treat staff fairly, they’ll be more likely to step in

29
00:02:12,775 --> 00:02:15,830
and help when extra staff are needed.

30
00:02:15,830 --> 00:02:21,670
Keeping staff happy has other tangible benefits for the business.

31
00:02:21,670 --> 00:02:24,850
Take the Dunwich Hotel as an example.

32
00:02:24,850 --> 00:02:31,151
It had been experiencing a problem with staff complaints and in order to deal

33
00:02:31,151 --> 00:02:36,590
with this, invested in staff training and improved staff conditions.

34
00:02:36,590 --> 00:02:40,003
Not only did the level of complaints fall,

35
00:02:40,003 --> 00:02:46,689
but they also noticed a significant increase in the amount each customer spent during

36
00:02:46,689 --> 00:02:47,070
their stay.

37
00:02:47,070 --> 00:02:54,760
They have now introduced a customer loyalty scheme which is going really well.

38
00:02:54,760 --> 00:03:00,013
Now I’d like to look at some ways you can reduce staff turnover in

39
00:03:00,013 --> 00:03:01,456
your hotels,

40
00:03:01,456 --> 00:03:06,480
and I’ll do this by giving some examples of hotels where I’ve done some

41
00:03:06,480 --> 00:03:07,190
training recently.

42
00:03:07,190 --> 00:03:14,870
The Sun Club received feedback which showed that staff thought managers didn’t value their

43
00:03:14,870 --> 00:03:14,920
opinions.

44
00:03:14,920 --> 00:03:20,101
They weren’t made to feel they were partners who were contributing to the success

45
00:03:20,101 --> 00:03:21,550
of the business as a whole.

46
00:03:21,550 --> 00:03:24,460
This situation has changed.

47
00:03:24,460 --> 00:03:31,066
Junior staff at all levels are regularly invited to meetings where their ideas are

48
00:03:31,066 --> 00:03:31,970
welcomed.

49
00:03:31,970 --> 00:03:38,790
A year ago, The Portland recognised the need to invest in staff retention.

50
00:03:38,790 --> 00:03:45,870
Their first step was to introduce a scheme for recognising talent amongst their employees.

51
00:03:45,870 --> 00:03:52,653
The hope is that organising training for individuals with management potential will encourage them

52
00:03:52,653 --> 00:03:54,680
to stay with the business.

53
00:03:54,680 --> 00:04:04,097
At Bluewater, managers decided to recognise 50 high achievers from across the company’s huge

54
00:04:04,097 --> 00:04:04,760
hotel chain.

55
00:04:04,760 --> 00:04:10,770
As a reward, they’re sent on an all-expenses-paid trip abroad every year.

56
00:04:10,770 --> 00:04:14,363
Fun is an important element in the trips,

57
00:04:14,363 --> 00:04:19,100
but there’s also the opportunity to learn something useful.

58
00:04:19,100 --> 00:04:25,301
This year’s trip included a visit to a brewery, where staff learned about the

59
00:04:25,301 --> 00:04:27,870
new beer that would be served in the hotel.

60
00:04:27,870 --> 00:04:34,970
Pentlow Hotels identified that retention of junior reception staff was an issue.

61
00:04:34,970 --> 00:04:40,364
In order to encourage them to see that working in a hotel could be worthwhile

62
00:04:40,364 --> 00:04:46,460
and rewarding, with good prospects, they introduced a management programme.

63
00:04:46,460 --> 00:04:52,434
These staff were given additional responsibilities and the chance to work in various roles

64
00:04:52,434 --> 00:04:53,480
in the hotel.

65
00:04:53,480 --> 00:04:58,730
Green Planet wanted to be seen as a caring employer.

66
00:04:58,730 --> 00:05:05,775
To make life easier for staff, many of whom had childcare responsibilities, the hotel

67
00:05:05,775 --> 00:05:09,930
began issuing vouchers to help cover the cost of childcare.

68
00:05:09,930 --> 00:05:16,597
Louise Marsh at The Amesbury has one of the best staff retention rates in

69
00:05:16,597 --> 00:05:17,320
the business.

70
00:05:17,320 --> 00:05:24,144
Since she joined the company, she has made a huge effort to achieve this by creating a co-operative

71
00:05:24,144 --> 00:05:25,870
and supportive environment.

72
00:05:25,870 --> 00:05:31,490
For her, the staff are part of a large family where everyone is valued.

73
00:05:31,490 --> 00:05:34,000
OK, now I’d like to …
